ec: bound stripe_reuse search and filter mismatched stripes early

stripe_reuse() searches BTREE_ID_lru (BCH_LRU_STRIPE_FRAGMENTATION, 2)
for partially filled stripes to reuse when ec_should_reuse_stripe() is
true (e.g. when any device is low on space, free space <= 40%).
However, on filesystems with multiple tiers or large numbers of fragmented
stripes, this search has two critical flaws:

1. stripe_reuse() has no iteration cap. It walks every single key in the
   LRU fragmentation list across the entire filesystem.
2. In get_old_stripe(), each key is looked up in BTREE_ID_stripes under an
   intent lock, and bch2_disk_label_ec_rw_member_devs() + can_widen demotion
   math are executed BEFORE checking if the stripe matches the allocation's
   tier label, algorithm, or redundancy. may_reuse_stripe() then rejects
   the candidate because old->disk_label != new->disk_label.

On pools with multiple tiers (e.g. CMR + SMR), writes to one tier spin in
stripe_reuse() examining thousands of mismatched stripes from the other tier.
The writeback flush worker (flush-bcachefs-*) gets pinned at 100% CPU in
get_old_stripe, collapsing writeback throughput to <1 MB/s, pinning dirty
pages in meminfo, and throttling all writers in balance_dirty_pages.

Fix:
1. In get_old_stripe(), check old.v->disk_label, algorithm, and nr_redundant
   immediately after peeking the stripe key. Skip mismatched stripes before
   doing device mask lookups or btree modifications.
2. In stripe_reuse(), bound the search by capping candidate evaluations at
   128 matching-tier candidates and total LRU entries visited at 512. If no
   reusable stripe is found, return -BCH_ERR_stripe_alloc_blocked so
   __stripe_alloc_or_reuse() falls through to new_stripe_alloc_buckets().

--- a/fs/data/ec/create.c
+++ b/fs/data/ec/create.c
@@ -1622,7 +1622,8 @@
 
 static int get_old_stripe(struct btree_trans *trans,
 			  struct ec_stripe_new *new,
-			  u64 idx)
+			  u64 idx,
+			  bool *is_candidate)
 {
 	struct bch_fs *c = trans->c;
 
@@ -1656,6 +1657,18 @@
 	}
 
 	/*
+	 * Cheap check first: skip stripes with mismatching label, algorithm,
+	 * or redundancy before doing expensive member device / can_widen math.
+	 */
+	if (old.v->disk_label		!= new->new_stripe.key.v.disk_label ||
+	    old.v->algorithm		!= new->new_stripe.key.v.algorithm ||
+	    old.v->nr_redundant		!= new->new_stripe.key.v.nr_redundant)
+		return 0;
+
+	if (is_candidate)
+		*is_candidate = true;
+
+	/*
 	 * Demote can_widen if the RW-member set for this disk_label has shrunk
 	 * since the last reconcile_scan_stripes pass: a stale-high value would
 	 * keep a non-widenable stripe at the top of the LRU, blocking real
@@ -1805,14 +1818,34 @@
 	struct bch_fs *c = trans->c;
 	struct bkey_s_c lru_k;
 	int ret = 0;
+	unsigned nr_candidates = 0;
+	unsigned nr_visited = 0;
+	const unsigned max_candidates = 128;
+	const unsigned max_visited = 512;
 
 	for_each_btree_key_max_norestart(trans, lru_iter, BTREE_ID_lru,
 			lru_pos(BCH_LRU_STRIPE_FRAGMENTATION, 2, 0),
 			lru_pos(BCH_LRU_STRIPE_FRAGMENTATION, 2, LRU_TIME_MAX),
 			0, lru_k, ret) {
-		ret = get_old_stripe(trans, s, lru_k.k->p.offset);
+		bool is_candidate = false;
+
+		nr_visited++;
+		ret = get_old_stripe(trans, s, lru_k.k->p.offset, &is_candidate);
 		if (ret)
 			break;
+
+		if (is_candidate)
+			nr_candidates++;
+
+		/*
+		 * Bound the search: reuse is an optimization. If we haven't found
+		 * a viable candidate within max_candidates (or after visiting
+		 * max_visited total keys), fall through to fresh stripe allocation
+		 * rather than pinning the writeback thread and holding
+		 * stripe_head_lock across thousands of btree keys.
+		 */
+		if (nr_candidates >= max_candidates || nr_visited >= max_visited)
+			break;
 	}
 	if (ret <= 0)
 		return ret ?: bch_err_throw(c, stripe_alloc_blocked);
